{{NZelectorateShortDesc|current=yes|type=general|region=Otago}}

'''Taieri''' is a parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]] in the [[Otago]] region of New Zealand, initially from 1866 to 1911, and was later recreated during the 2019/20 electoral redistribution ahead of the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 election]].<ref>{{Cite web | url=https://vote.nz/boundary-review-2019-2020/ | title=Boundary Review 2019/20 &#124; Vote NZ}}</ref><ref name="TVNZ 17 April 2020">{{cite news |last1=Whyte |first1=Anna |title=New electorate revealed, as raft of boundary changes announced prior to election 2020 |url=https://www.tvnz.co.nz/one-news/new-zealand/new-electorate-revealed-raft-boundary-changes-announced-prior-election-2020 |access-date=3 July 2020 |work=[[1News]] |date=17 April 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200417220424/https://www.tvnz.co.nz/one-news/new-zealand/new-electorate-revealed-raft-boundary-changes-announced-prior-election-2020 |archive-date=17 April 2020}}</ref>

==Population centres==
===First incarnation===
In the 1865 electoral redistribution, the [[New Zealand House of Representatives|House of Representatives]] focussed its review of electorates to [[South Island]] electorates only, as the [[Otago gold rush]] had caused significant population growth, and a redistribution of the existing population. Fifteen additional South Island electorates were created, including Taieri, and the number of Members of Parliament was increased by 13 to 70.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=36}}

This electorate was based on the town of [[Mosgiel]].{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=37}}

===Second incarnation===
{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Taieri.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
The seat was recreated for the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 general election]] by renaming the electorate of [[Dunedin South]] and amending its borders, particularly with a large area around the [[Clutha River]] and [[South Otago]] added from [[Clutha-Southland]].<ref>{{cite web|date=17 April 2020|title=Report of the Representation Commission 2020|url=https://vote.nz/assets/Boundary-Review/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2020.pdf|access-date=18 April 2020}}</ref><ref name="TVNZ 17 April 2020" /> This is due to a rate of population growth below the South Island average in [[Dunedin]] that means it can no longer fully support two electorates.<ref>{{cite web|title=Map of electorates for 2020 and 2023|url=https://www.vote.nz/map/index.html?id=2&modified=2020110110227}}</ref> The current electorate of Taieri has an area stretching from [[South Dunedin]] to [[Balclutha, New Zealand|Balclutha]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://vote.nz/boundary-review-2019-2020/what-are-the-main-changes/ |title=2019–2020 boundary review; What are the main changes?}}</ref>

The previous electorate of Dunedin South had been held by the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]] since its creation in 1996, and specifically by [[Clare Curran]] since 2008. However, the new boundaries for Taieri include more rural areas,<ref>{{Cite web|date=2020-06-24|title=Mark Patterson chosen to represent NZ First for Taieri electorate in general election|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/121932964/mark-patterson-chosen-to-represent-nz-first-for-taieri-electorate-in-general-election|access-date=2020-07-08|website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |language=en}}</ref> and Curran announced in 2019 that she would not be seeking reelection in 2020.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Walls|first=Jason|date=2019-08-27|title=Labour MP Clare Curran steps down – a year after she was sacked from Cabinet|language=en-NZ|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12262363|access-date=2020-07-08|issn=1170-0777}}</ref>

==History==

The Taieri electorate was first established for the [[1866 New Zealand general election|1866 general election]] for the [[4th New Zealand Parliament]].

[[Donald Reid (politician born 1833)|Donald Reid]] was the first representative.<ref name="1866 general election">{{cite news|title=The New Parliament |url=http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=NOT18660419.2.14 |access-date=11 July 2010 |work=[[North Otago Times]] |volume=VI |issue=113 |date=19 April 1866 |page=1}}</ref> He resigned in 1869 and was succeeded by [[Henry Howorth (New Zealand politician)|Henry Howorth]]. Reid was re-elected at the [[1871 New Zealand general election|1871 general election]], and elected unopposed in the [[1875–1876 New Zealand general election|1875 general election]] that was held on 29 December.<ref name="1875/76 Port Chalmers and Taieri elections">{{cite news|title=The Taieri Election |url=http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=OW18760101.2.28 |access-date=27 July 2010 |work=[[Otago Witness]] |date=1 January 1876|issue=1257 |page=9}}</ref> Reid resigned once again in 1878,<ref name="DNZB Reid sr">{{cite web | url=http://www.dnzb.govt.nz/dnzb/alt_essayBody.asp?essayID=2R13 | title= Reid, Donald 1833–1919 | last= Angus | first= John H. | date= 22 June 2007 |publisher=Dictionary of New Zealand Biography | access-date= 27 July 2010}}</ref> and was succeeded by [[William Cutten]]. Reid's son, also called [[Donald Reid (politician born 1855)|Donald Reid]], represented the electorate 1902–08.<ref name="NZETC Reid jr">{{cite book |title=The Cyclopedia of New Zealand : Otago & Southland Provincial Districts |year=1905 |url=https://nzetc.victoria.ac.nz/tm/scholarly/tei-Cyc04Cycl-t1-body1-d2-d25-d4.html#name-428209-mention |author=Cyclopedia Company Limited |access-date= 27 July 2010 |location=Christchurch |chapter=Barristers And Solicitors}}</ref>

All other members retired at the end of their representation. When the electorate was abolished in 1911, [[Thomas Mackenzie]] stood successfully for [[Egmont (New Zealand electorate)|Egmont]].

